{
  "_what": "The residue countdown for the two-harness window (ADR 0003, docs/decisions/). This is what is LEFT of the old probe harness, recorded PER FILE rather than as four totals. HarnessResidueTests derives the same map from the source tree every run and holds the two against each other; the four headline numbers - probeFiles, probeLines, ciAllRows, guardFilesOutsideTheCount - are DERIVED from this map, never stored. Per file rather than per total so that a conversion PR deletes its own key instead of editing a number every other conversion PR also edits: two conversions of different families then merge cleanly. THREE MEASURES GATE, exact equality in both directions - growth is a failing test, and a shrink that is not recorded here is one too: the probeFiles key set, the ciAllRows count on each file, and the guardsOutsideTheCountedFiles key set. A fourth, standaloneCiSteps, gates the same way: a guard CI runs as its own workflow step is residue too, and would otherwise be invisible to every count here. ONE ONLY REPORTS: the lines value is derived and printed beside them, never asserted. W7 is not complete while any gated number is above zero.",

  "_how": "probeFiles = every *Probe*.cs under src/housecarl-generator (recursive, bin/obj excluded), keyed by its repo-relative forward-slashed path. lines = File.ReadAllLines().Length for that file - REPORT-ONLY. ciAllRows = how many of the ci-all roster's verbs have their entry point in that file, derived from CiAll.ProbeHosts (the [CiProbe]-attributed methods themselves, not a table and not a name convention) - GATED. standaloneCiSteps = the same count for verbs CI runs as their own workflow step instead of inside ci-all, flagged Standalone on the attribute; the key is omitted where it is zero - GATED. guardsOutsideTheCountedFiles = the host files of guards that the *Probe*.cs glob does not count, which is the one honest answer to 'what does CI run' for a guard living outside this project: WriteEngine.cs is product code in src/housecarl-core hosting two coerce verbs, and folding its 4,000 lines into probeFiles would inflate the countdown with code that is not residue - GATED, and reported by name. Totals: probeFiles = the number of keys, probeLines = the sum of lines, ciAllRows = the sum of ciAllRows across BOTH maps and independently equal to ci-all's own N/N summary line, guardFilesOutsideTheCount = the number of keys in the second map.",

  "_why_lines_only_report": "The line count moves in both directions on ordinary in-place guard edits, which the two-harness rule requires: if you are editing an existing guard, you edit it where it already lives. Gating it made the correct act a failing build, so it was ruled report-only (ADR 0003). It is still recorded per file, and the test prints every file whose real count has drifted from the value here, so a conversion PR can refresh what it touches without anybody chasing a total. A stale lines value is information going out of date; a stale gated value would be the countdown going quiet, which is the difference.",

  "_raised_once": "Raising any gated number is legal and deliberate, and it is meant to be visible: the raise and the reason land in the same commit, argued, once. That is what keeps this a countdown rather than headroom. The one edit that is neither a conversion nor a raise is a RELOCATION - moving a guard's entry point from one old-harness file to another, which is a legal in-place edit that leaves the ciAllRows total unchanged while two per-file values move. It costs two lines here rather than none. That is the price of per-file resolution, and it is worth it: the alternative is one shared total that every conversion PR conflicts on.",

  "_lowered_by": "A conversion PR: it deletes the probe, adds the tests, and deletes that probe's key from this file in the same commit. Nothing else in this file moves, which is why two conversion PRs converting different families do not conflict here.",

